# Your scorecards. Ignored on purpose: they name real players, and a fork or a
# pull request would publish them. The folder itself is kept, along with the
# note explaining what goes in it, so a fresh clone still has somewhere to put
# the PDFs. `python3 tools/demo.py` also writes here, and its fictional season
# is likewise nothing to commit.
#
# If your club *wants* its cards in the repository -- a private repo, or results
# that are published anyway -- delete these four lines.
/data/*
!/data/.gitkeep
!/data/README.md
!/data/*.md

# Your own config.toml, for the same reason as data/: [captains], [player_names]
# and [aliases] are lists of real people's names, and a fork or a pull request
# would publish them. `python3 build.py` writes this file for you from the cards,
# so a fresh clone is never without one for long. config.example.toml, which
# documents every setting and invents every name in it, is committed instead.
#
# Delete this line if you want your settings in the repository — a private repo,
# or a second machine to clone onto, are both good reasons.
/config.toml

# What `python3 build.py --discover` proposes beside a config that is there
# already: config.proposed.toml beside config.toml, and the same beside any
# other file named with --config, public.toml among them. It holds every name
# the file beside it holds, for the reason above, and the next --discover
# writes it again, so there is nothing in it to keep.
*.proposed.toml

# Generated dashboard. Rebuilt from data/ by `python3 build.py`, so it is not
# committed by default. Delete this line if you want the built site in git —
# publishing to GitHub Pages from the repo is a legitimate reason to.
/site/

# What tools/scheduled.py keeps between runs. A report names every player.
/reports/

# The copy without names that README.md builds into public/, the public.toml it
# builds from, and the reports-public/ its scheduled line keeps. public/ is
# rebuilt from data/, as site/ is, and the other two name players, as
# config.toml and reports/ do, so none of it is committed by default. Delete the
# /public/ line if you want that copy in git: publishing to GitHub Pages from
# the repo is a legitimate reason to.
/public/
/public.toml
/reports-public/

# What `python3 tools/demo.py --seasons multi` writes beside build.py: two
# fictional seasons, and the config that builds them. Nothing to commit, as the
# plain demo's season in data/ is not.
/demo-seasons/
/demo-seasons.toml

# What `python3 tools/release.py publish` writes: a release's archive and its
# notes. The workflow makes them afresh from the tag every time, and the archive
# is the whole project, so nothing in here is anything to commit.
/dist/

# Design and development notes. Working documents, not part of the product.
/notes/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# A virtual environment made beside build.py out of habit. The tool needs
# nothing installed, and before Python 3.13 `python3 -m venv .venv` writes
# no ignore file of its own, so the next `git add .` would commit pip.
/.venv/

# Editors and OS
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/
